  "id": "DrugBank_DB01110 - MONDO_5915",
  "verbalization": "Miconazole, a broad-spectrum antifungal agent, is linked to Pityriasis Versicolor through a chain of indications and off-label use. The evidence suggests that Miconazole is used off-label for Tinea Infection and Dermatophytosis, both of which are fungal infections that can manifest as Pityriasis Versicolor. Naftifine, a commonly used antifungal, is indicated for these conditions and is frequently used off-label for Pityriasis Versicolor. This creates a pathway where Miconazole, through its indication for Tinea Infection and Dermatophytosis, indirectly influences the treatment of Pityriasis Versicolor via Naftifine. The shared antifungal mechanism of action between Miconazole and Naftifine supports the hypothesis that Miconazole may contribute to the treatment of Pityriasis Versicolor through a mechanism that is not directly indicated in its primary use. The evidence is consistent and complementary, highlighting a potential role of Miconazole in the management of Pityriasis Versicolor through its antifungal properties, even when not explicitly indicated for this condition.",
